WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.839
Task statement 1.4.

2
00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:06.702
Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns.

3
00:00:06.702 --> 00:00:14.665
If a support agent can issue a refund before checking who it is talking to, one bad run is a real financial loss.

4
00:00:16.065 --> 00:00:22.290
Think about a sign that says please show your ID, versus a turnstile that will not open without it.

5
00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:23.925
A prompt rule is the sign.

6
00:00:23.925 --> 00:00:27.823
Claude follows it almost every time, but almost is not always.

7
00:00:27.823 --> 00:00:35.180
When an order of steps is required, like verifying a customer before touching money, you build the turnstile in code.

8
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:37.874
Here is a small gate.

9
00:00:37.874 --> 00:00:45.330
We keep track of sessions where the customer has been verified, for example by marking it after the verify tool succeeds.

10
00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.904
A pre tool use hook on the refund tool checks that record.

11
00:00:48.904 --> 00:00:55.929
If verification has not happened, the call is denied, and the reason tells Claude exactly which step to run first.

12
00:00:57.329 --> 00:01:00.624
Customers often ask for several things in one message.

13
00:01:00.624 --> 00:01:05.810
Split them into separate items, resolve each one, and check that nothing was dropped.

14
00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:15.512
When a case must go to a person, hand off a short structured summary: who the customer is, the root cause, the amounts, what was tried, and what you recommend.

15
00:01:15.512 --> 00:01:18.257
Nobody wants to read a forty turn transcript.

16
00:01:19.657 --> 00:01:24.776
A customer writes in about a damaged blender, a new address, and their loyalty points.

17
00:01:24.776 --> 00:01:28.586
The gate makes sure identity is checked before anything changes.

18
00:01:28.586 --> 00:01:32.158
The agent fixes the address and answers the points question.

19
00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:40.372
The refund is above the agent's limit, so it escalates with a structured summary, and the human can approve it without rereading the chat.

20
00:01:41.772 --> 00:01:48.185
On the exam, stronger wording and more examples are tempting but wrong when an order must always hold.

21
00:01:48.185 --> 00:01:49.819
The answer is a code gate.

22
00:01:49.819 --> 00:01:54.283
For escalations, prefer the structured summary over the raw transcript.

23
00:01:54.283 --> 00:02:01.953
And when a message carries several requests, the right design splits them and confirms each one is resolved before ending.

24
00:02:03.353 --> 00:02:03.918
To recap.

25
00:02:03.918 --> 00:02:07.560
If an order of steps must always hold, enforce it in code.

26
00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:11.641
Block the risky tools until the prerequisite has really happened.

27
00:02:11.641 --> 00:02:15.157
Break multi-part requests into items and close each one.

28
00:02:15.157 --> 00:02:23.193
And when a person takes over, give them a structured summary with the customer, the cause, the amounts and a recommended action.

